---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About | Aaron's Dev Experiments">
	<section class="topic-header">
		<p class="eyebrow">Behind the Code</p>

		<h1>About This Site</h1>

		<p class="intro">
			A personal technical notebook for the projects and experiments I
			spend my free time working through.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="about-content">
		<h2>About Me</h2>

		<div class="about-intro">
			<img class="about-photo" src="/images/me.jpg" alt="Aaron" />

			<div>
				<p>
					I'm Aaron, a construction worker turned retailer turned
					computer science graduate, now trying to turn all of that
					into a career in software development.
				</p>

				<p>
					I've always enjoyed building things and working with my
					hands. Programming was a natural extension of that. It lets
					me take things apart, see how the pieces fit together, and
					use those pieces to build increasingly interesting and
					complex things.
				</p>

				<p>
					I continue trying to grow my skills, and this site is a part
					of that. Lately my focus has been on graphics, terrain
					systems, architecture, and whatever technical problem
					happens to catch my attention.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h2>What I'm Working On</h2>

		<p>
			Most of the material here currently comes from Plane Voxel Lab, a
			C++ / OpenGL project where I was exploring terrain representation,
			rendering, spatial systems, and editable world geometry. That's a
			fancy way of saying that I was trying to lay the foundation for a 3D
			building/crafting game, starting with the terrain.
		</p>

		<p>
			The site is designed to be intentionally broader than that
			particular project. I plan on continuing these experiments—following
			some to more interesting conclusions, while ending others as an
			article. The long-term goal is to build and publish a game along the
			way, and I wanted room here for all of that.
		</p>

		<p>
			I may also add some of my other hobby stuff on here too, if it's
			interesting enough to write about. Or, if someone might find some
			use from it.
		</p>

		<h2>Why Document It?</h2>

		<p>
			There are a few reasons for the site. One is to put something of
			myself out there in the world. I like the idea of people with
			similar interests seeing what I'm working on. If it helps them solve
			a similar problem or if someone wants to connect and collaborate,
			that would be really cool.
		</p>

		<p>
			It also helps me. The process of writing about these projects forces
			me to sit down and pore over the details—what worked, what didn't,
			why it worked, etc. It gives me a way to organize my thoughts
			outside of the code itself.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
